   "who": "FRZWLF",
   "text": "## 🔐 Security review\n\nScope: `docs/08-security.md` T1/T2/T5/T10 against the diff of `feat/issue-2`. Evidence reused:\n`npm ci && npm test` re-run here → **86/86 pass, exit 0**; the 18 mutations in the PR body are\ntaken as given and not repeated. Two probes of my own, both on a throwaway database.\n\n**What held up.** Every repository statement binds `scope.orgId` first; I read all seven\nrepositories plus `src/db/instance.ts` and found no query without it, and no SQL outside\n`src/db/`. Token verification is the right shape: version inside the MAC, `timingSafeEqual`,\nsignature checked *before* `exp`/`iat`, an unknown `kid` still pays for one HMAC, integer\nseconds only, a 4096-byte bound, `parseClaims` refusing anything but exactly five typed keys.\nForgery attempts (truncated MAC, all-`A` MAC, `v2.` swap, a fourth segment) were all refused.\n`ConfigError` names the variable and never the value; the process exits 1 without a secret.\n`.env`, `data/` and `*.db` are gitignored and nothing secret is committed. No dependencies, so\nno supply-chain surface. **No 🔴.**\n\n### 🟡 Nits\n\n1. **Signature encoding is malleable** — `verifyToken` compares the *decoded* 32 bytes, and\n   `Buffer.from(sig, 'base64url')` ignores the last character's two unused bits. **Verified:\n   4 distinct token strings verify for the same claims** (tail `…s`, `…p`, `…q`, `…r`). No\n   impact today because nothing keys on the token string — but G-013 (revocation, high\n   priority) is naturally implemented as a denylist of token hashes, and that denylist would\n   be bypassable 4× over. One line closes it: also require\n   `signatureB64 === expected.toString('base64url')` after the `timingSafeEqual`.\n2. **`writeAudit` does not scope its foreign references (T10, D-007).** `requisitionId` and\n   `actorPersonId` are written verbatim; the FKs are `REFERENCES requisitions(id)` /\n   `people(id)` by id alone, not `(org_id, id)`. **Verified:** a scope for org B wrote an\n   audit line under `org_id = B` pointing at org A's requisition and person, and the\n   `BEFORE UPDATE`/`BEFORE DELETE` triggers then make it permanent. It does **not** leak —\n   org A's `listForRequisition` still returns 0 rows — but every other write path here\n   (`insertDraft`, `costCentres.insert`, `grantRole`) validates its references against the\n   scope, and the one append-only table is the one that doesn't. A `selectRequisition.get(scope.orgId, id)`\n   guard, or composite `(org_id, id)` uniques + FKs, makes split 02 unable to get this wrong.\n3. **The leak table covers repositories, not methods.** `peopleRepo.list`,\n   `catalogueRepo.list`, `auditRepo.list`, `requisitionsRepo.list({buyerPersonId})` and the\n   cross-org *buyer* / *catalogue item* write paths are outside the 13 cases —\n   `test/db/tenancy-leak.test.ts:204` counts repositories. I exercised all six as org B and\n   **all six are correct today** (`[]`, `0 rows`, `not_found: buyer`, `not_found: line 1:\n   catalogue item`), so this is coverage, not a leak — but D-014 calls this suite mandatory\n   and a new method currently joins it unasserted.\n4. **Log redaction is key-name only** — `/token|secret|authorization|password|cookie/i`\n   misses `bearer`, `authHeader`, `credential`, and any field whose *value* happens to be a\n   token. Cheap hardening before the HTTP layer lands (T5): also redact a string value\n   matching `^v1\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.`.\n\nNot my lane, flagged for the record: the size deviation (4 164 lines vs. a 2.6–3.0k estimate\nand a named cut point) is a process call for the quality review and FRZWLF, not a security one.\n\nVERDICT: findings (0 red, 4 nits)\n",
