   "text": "## 🔐 Security review\n\nRe-review of fix round 1 (`9e29d6b`), scoped to what the fix touched: the canonical-signature\ncheck, the `writeAudit` organisation validation, the redaction walker and the frozen key view.\n`npm ci && npm test` — 99/99 green.\n\n### The four round-1 fixes hold\n\n- **Canonical signature (🟡 1)** — closed correctly. The equality on the *string* runs only after\n  `timingSafeEqual` has already returned true, so the malleability check adds no timing channel of\n  its own, and `BASE64URL_RE` still gates the input before either. The test derives the three\n  malleable spellings from the alphabet instead of hard-coding them, so it fails loudly if the\n  encoding stops being malleable rather than passing vacuously.\n- **`writeAudit` org validation (🟡 2)** — closed correctly. Both reference checks run on the same\n  connection as `tx`, so rows written earlier in the same transaction are visible (the positive\n  case in `tenancy-leak.test.ts` proves it), and there is no check-then-write window. Turning the\n  return into `Result<AuditLine>` introduces no ignored refusal: `writeAudit` has no caller in\n  `src/` or `scripts/` yet, so the next slice inherits a type error rather than a silent audit gap.\n- **Redaction walker (🟡 4)** — cycle-safe. There is no `WeakSet`, but `MAX_DEPTH = 6` is checked\n  before either recursive branch, so a self-referential field terminates at a `[redacted]` subtree\n  instead of hanging; I confirmed the depth counter is threaded through both `redact` and\n  `redactValue` (start 0, `+1` per level).\n- **Cross-org leak table (🟡 3)** — the four added read cases and the three write cases cover the\n  repositories the branch ships, and `CASES.length >= 13` keeps the table from silently shrinking.\n\n### 🟡 1 — `freezeMap`'s `forEach` hands out the live, unfrozen `Map`\n\n`src/config.ts`: the view is frozen, but `forEach` passes `inner` as the callback's third argument:\n\n```ts\nforEach: (callback, thisArg?) => {\n  for (const [key, value] of inner) {\n    callback.call(thisArg, value, key, inner);   // ← the mutable Map escapes\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`ReadonlyMap` is only a type, and at the `dist/` boundary it is gone — which is the exact premise\nof the fix. Probe (one, as allowed), against the built config:\n\n```\nthird arg is a live Map: true  frozen? false\nAFTER MUTATION kids: [ '1', '2' ]  size: 2\nkid 1 now: attacker-key\n```\n\nSo `config.tokenKeys.forEach((_v, _k, m) => m.set('1', attackerKey))` replaces the active signing\nand verification key on a config that `startup.test.ts` has just asserted is frozen.\n\nNot a 🔴: nothing in `src/` or `scripts/` calls `.forEach` on `tokenKeys` (only\n`migrate.ts` uses `forEach`, on a local array), and reaching this needs in-process code execution,\nwhich is already game over. It is a defeated hardening control (D-018), not an exploit — but it is\nthe one mutator the new view forgot, and the round-1 test asserts `set`/`delete`/`clear` are gone\nwithout covering this path. Fix: pass a frozen self-reference (or the view itself) as the third\nargument, and extend the `startup.test.ts` case to assert the third argument has no `set`.\n\n### 🟡 2 — the value-shape redaction only knows the `v1.x.y` token shape\n\n`src/log.ts`: `SECRET_VALUE_RE = /^v\\d+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/` catches Requisit's own\ntokens, which is the right first move. But a header bag logged under a name the key regex does not\nmatch — `{ headers: { 'x-api-key': 'sk-live-…' } }`, or an upstream `Authorization` value carried\nas `{ proxy: { upstreamAuth: 'Bearer eyJ…' } }` (`auth` alone does not match `auth_?header`) —\nstill prints in full. Hardening only, and no such field is logged on this branch; worth a note on\nG-016 so the next transport slice does not discover it by leaking one.\n\nNothing else in scope: no new dependency, no new listener, no secret in a message or an error path,\nand the `--ttl` cap and `roundHalfUp` rewrite have no security surface beyond the bound itself.\n\nVERDICT: findings (0 red, 2 nits)\n",
